{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas` CLI defines and parses its commands and options with `clap`, and the tests cover that.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `Cargo.toml:45` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `src/main.rs:1` imports `clap::{ArgGroup, Parser, Subcommand}`.\n- `src/main.rs` defines the CLI with clap derive types, each marked `{impl}` for `requirements.cli.clap`:\n  - `Cli` (`#[derive(Parser)]`).\n  - `Command` (`#[derive(Subcommand)]`) with `mdbook`, `setup`, `status`, `info`, `author` (including the `author-evidence` alias), the hidden `hook`, and `add-input`.\n  - `MdbookCommand` and `HookCommand`.\n- `main` parses with `Cli::parse()` (line 96), also marked `{impl}`.\n- A search for `env::args` and `clap` found no hand-rolled argument parsing anywhere else.\n- Clap-level constraints are declared with `ArgGroup` and `conflicts_with`. They cover the exactly-one target and mode for `add-input`, and the subject-or-`--all` choice for `author`.\n- The other behavior in `main` (workspace config loading, the mdBook preprocessor, setup, status, info, authoring, the hook) is marked with the requirements that specify it. I found no unspecified behavior marked only under this requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/cli.rs:43` `clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors` runs the real binary and checks:\n  - The `--help` usage line lists every public subcommand and hides `hook`.\n  - `status --help` shows `--all` and `--verbose`.\n  - The `author-evidence` alias resolves to `author`.\n  - An unknown option, a missing required argument, and conflicting arguments each exit with code 2 and print a clap usage message.\n- `tests/commands.rs:928` `add_input_requires_one_target_and_mode_and_resets_changed_records` also carries `{test}` for this requirement. It checks that invalid target and mode combinations produce clap usage errors with exit code 2 and that nothing is written. It also checks that valid combinations parse and run.\n\nTogether these cover the main points in the design space for \"uses clap\": help output, subcommands, aliases, hidden commands, required and conflicting argument groups, and usage-error exit behavior.\n\nNo gaps and no FIXMEs.",
